</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/samsung-not-cooling"
  eyebrow="Samsung Refrigerator Not Cooling · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Samsung Refrigerator Not Cooling? First, Rule Out Demo Mode"
  subtitle="If it's lit but not cold and shows OF OF, that's demo mode — not a breakdown. Here's how to exit it, the real no-cool causes on Twin Cooling models, and how long your food stays safe."
  heroImageTitle="Samsung Refrigerator Not Cooling Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Lit up but not cold? Check for OF OF first.</h2>

    <p><strong>Before you troubleshoot a Samsung that won't cool, rule out a non-fault: if the panel is lit but nothing's cooling and you see OF OF (or O FF, or scrolling bars), that's Cooling Off / demo mode — the showroom setting that disables cooling, ice, and the dispenser. It's not a breakdown, and people trigger it by accident. Exit by holding the two arrow buttons together about eight seconds until a chime. If there's no OF OF and it's genuinely warm, the causes are the evaporator fan ("freezer cold, fridge warm"), a defrost system iced over, dirty condenser coils, or a thermistor or board. Coils and airflow are yours; the sealed system is a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Food is on a clock:</strong> per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door closed. Keep the doors shut, move critical items to a cooler with ice if you can, and get a tech out before that window closes. Full food-safety details are in the section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 DEMO MODE (differentiator) -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · OF OF — demo mode, not a breakdown</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The most common "dead Samsung" that isn't dead.</h2>

    <p>The single most-panicked Samsung "not cooling" call is a fridge in <strong>Cooling Off / demo mode</strong>, shown as <strong>OF OF</strong>, <strong>O FF</strong>, or scrolling bars on the display. It's the mode stores use to light up the panel without running the compressor, so the screen works but nothing cools, no ice is made, and the dispenser is dead. Owners switch it on by accident — often while cleaning the panel — and assume the refrigerator failed.</p>

    <p>To exit, press and hold the two arrow buttons (the left and right, often labeled with the freezer and fridge controls) together for about <strong>eight seconds</strong> until you hear a chime. Some models use a different combination, so if that doesn't work, check your model's manual for the demo-mode exit. Once you're out, cooling resumes on its own. It costs nothing and it's the first thing to check — which is exactly why we lead with it. The full Samsung code list, including OF OF, is on our <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/samsung-error-codes/">Samsung refrigerator error codes</a> guide.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Samsung stops cooling</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The real causes, once demo mode is ruled out.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan — and Twin Cooling</h3>
      <p>Samsung's Twin Cooling Plus models have separate evaporators and fans for the fridge and freezer, so one compartment can warm while the other stays cold. A failed fridge-side fan is the classic "freezer cold, fridge warm." A tech job inside the cabinet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Defrost system iced over</h3>
      <p>A failed defrost heater, sensor, or control lets frost build on the evaporator until air can't pass and cooling drops. The tell is a wall of frost behind the freezer's back panel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dirty condenser coils</h3>
      <p>Coils caked in dust can't shed the compressor's heat, so cooling fades — worse in an LA summer. This is the most common homeowner-fixable cause.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Thermistor or control board</h3>
      <p>A misreading temperature sensor or a failed board can stop the fridge from calling for cooling, and may show a Samsung code. Both are tech replacements.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Compressor / sealed system</h3>
      <p>If the compressor runs but doesn't cool or won't start, the sealed system is the fault — a gauges-and-tech diagnosis, and the higher-ticket end of the list.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Exit demo mode</strong> if you see OF OF — hold the two arrow buttons ~8 seconds.</li>
      <li><strong>Vacuum the condenser coils</strong> behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the interior vents</strong> so food isn't blocking airflow between compartments.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door seal and temperature setting,</strong> and pull the fridge a few inches off the wall to shed heat.</li>
      <li><strong>Power-cycle</strong> — unplug a minute, restart — then watch for improvement over a few hours.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> demo mode, coils, vents, seal, temperature, and a power-cycle are safe. Do not open the sealed refrigeration system, test the compressor, or replace the control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FOOD SAFETY -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Heat-wave food safety — when to call today</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A warm fridge is a clock. Here are the numbers.</h2>

    <p>A refrigerator that's stopped cooling is a race against the clock, and in an LA heat wave that clock runs faster. Per USDA and FDA guidance:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Refrigerator: about 4 hours</strong> of safe food with the door kept closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Freezer: roughly 48 hours full, 24 hours half-full,</strong> if the door stays shut.</li>
      <li><strong>Above 40°F for 2 hours or more → discard it.</strong> That covers meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, and leftovers.</li>
      <li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out</strong> — and never taste food to decide. Frozen food can be safely refrozen only if it still has ice crystals or is at 40°F or below.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>So once you've ruled out demo mode and the Samsung is genuinely warm, treat it as a same-day call. Keep the doors closed, move critical items to a cooler with ice, and get a tech out before the four-hour fridge window closes. We answer phones 24/7 and run same-day across most of the area. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Demo mode's off, coils are clean, still warm? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When demo mode is ruled out, the coils are clean, and the Samsung still won't cool, the fault is inside — the evaporator fan, the defrost system, a thermistor or board, or the sealed system. Our techs test each in order and carry the common Samsung fan motors and defrost parts for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a> guide (all brands), and if it's the fridge warm while the freezer's fine, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>. Codes: <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/samsung-error-codes/">Samsung refrigerator error codes</a>. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/samsung-refrigerator-repair/">Samsung refrigerator repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/irvine/refrigerator-repair/">Irvine</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Samsung refrigerator not cooling: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
